
🔐 What is Password Hashing?

Password hashing is a one-way cryptographic process that transforms a plaintext password into a fixed-length string of jumbled characters, known as a hash.
When you create an account on a website, secure systems do not save your actual password in their database. Instead, the system runs your password through a hashing algorithm (such as bcrypt, Argon2, or SHA-256) and stores only the resulting hash.

How Authentication Works:

1. Sign-up: You enter MyPassword123 → The system hashes it into a8f9c2... → Only a8f9c2... is saved.
2. Login: You enter MyPassword123 again → The system hashes it using the same algorithm → It compares the new hash to the stored hash. If they match, you are granted access.
Unlike encryption, which is designed to be reversed using a decryption key, hashing is strictly a one-way function. It is mathematically impossible to reverse-engineer a strong hash back into the original plaintext password.


Why Password Hashing Matters

• Eliminates Plaintext Risks: Storing passwords in clear text means a single data leak instantly hands cybercriminals the keys to every user account. Hashing converts credentials into a one-way, unreadable string.
• Stops Credential Reuse Exploits: Because people reuse the same password across multiple websites, leaking a plaintext password on one minor site compromises their accounts everywhere. Hashing mitigates direct exposure.
• One-Way Protection: Cryptographic hashing functions cannot be mathematically reversed to recover the original password.
• Defeats Precomputed Attacks via Salting: Modern hashing pairs with a unique random string called a "salt" for each user, which blocks attackers from using massive precomputed lookup tables (rainbow tables) to crack thousands of passwords at once.
• Resists Brute-Force Scaling: Purpose-built password hashing algorithms (bcrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2) are intentionally computationally expensive and slow down high-speed GPU cracking attempts.
• Regulatory Compliance: Security mandates like GDPR, HIPAA, and PCI DSS legally require organizations to protect user credentials securely rather than exposing them.
