🧂 What is Salting?

Salting is the security practice of adding a unique, random string of characters—called a salt—to a plaintext password before it is run through a hashing function.
If two users choose the exact same password (like Password123), their resulting hashes would normally be identical. Hackers exploit this using Rainbow Tables (massive, precompiled lists of common passwords and their hashes) to instantly crack databases. Salting completely prevents this by ensuring that even if two users have the same password, their final stored hashes will be completely different.

⚙️ The Salt Parameter in bcrypt.hash()

When you use a library like Node.js or Python to run a function like bcrypt.hash(password, salt), the salt parameter controls two critical security elements: the random salt string itself and the cost factor (work factor).

1. Generating the Salt String

In modern libraries, you rarely generate a manual text string. Instead, you tell bcrypt how secure the salt needs to be by passing a number called the rounds (usually 10 or 12).
• The library automatically creates a secure, mathematically random 22-character string using a cryptographically secure random number generator.
• This string is unique to that specific user and that specific password creation event.

2. The Cost Factor (Work Factor)

The number of rounds you pass into the salt generation function represents the cost factor. Bcrypt uses a formula of \(2^{\text{rounds}}\) to determine how many times it will repeatedly scramble the data.
• If you set the parameter to 12, bcrypt runs the hashing loop \(2^{12} = 4,096\) times.
• This is a deliberate "speed bump." It takes a fraction of a second for a legitimate user logging in, but it makes it incredibly slow and expensive for a hacker trying to guess millions of combinations using high-powered graphics cards (GPUs).

🔍 How Bcrypt Structurally Stores the Salt

Bcrypt makes management easy by automatically embedding the salt parameter right inside the final output string. You do not need a separate database column for the salt.
A standard bcrypt output looks like this:
\(\underbrace{\mathbf{\$2b\$}}_{\text{Algorithm\ ID}}\quad \underbrace{\mathbf{\$12\$}}_{\text{Cost\ Factor}}\quad \underbrace{\mathbf{R9h/cIQeNoX5L.bK/a1nFe}}_{\text{22-Character\ Random\ Salt}}\quad \underbrace{\mathbf{ZObW6qL3P2bM5K6i7j8}}_{\text{Scrambled\ Password\ Hash}}\)

How the System Verifies Your Login:

1. When you type your password to log in, the system fetches this full string from the database.
2. The verification function reads the first section to extract the exact Cost Factor and Random Salt that were used originally.
3. It applies that exact salt and cost factor to the password you just typed.
4. If the new calculation matches the final hash string, access is granted.
